{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfunuStzrAC8pqjjuxFTm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**KV: yes, in recent Wrangler 4.x versions, if you leave the ID out.** If a `kv_namespaces` binding has only a `binding` name and no `id`, `wrangler deploy` creates the namespace and links it. This is Wrangler's automatic resource provisioning, which also covers R2 and D1. If you specify an `id`, that namespace must already exist. Older Wrangler versions never auto-create, so you'd need to run `wrangler kv namespace create` first.\n\n**Queues: don't count on it.** As far as I know, queues aren't part of auto-provisioning, so a producer or consumer binding pointing at a missing queue makes the deploy fail. Create them first with `wrangler queues create <name>`.\n\nThis feature has been changing, so check `wrangler --version` and the current Cloudflare docs or changelog to confirm what your version does."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2252,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2252},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01809f23-f81a-4ed9-a1e2-dcb50b85aef0","uuid":"105dd8cc-ae7a-4b2c-8edc-d091261ac641","timestamp":"2026-10-11T04:08:24.311Z","request_id":"req_011CfunuShLSHmdvcw1ye4qD"}
